/-!
# The free model of the hash algebra

The theorems in `Merkle/Basic.lean` and `Merkle/Proof.lean` are quantified over *every*
type-class instance satisfying `Merkle.MerkleHash`. A theorem quantified over an empty class of
models would be vacuously true and worthless, so this file supplies a concrete, computable
model and proves that it satisfies the axioms. That single fact is what makes every security
theorem in the library non-vacuous.

## What is proved

* `Merkle.Free.Dig` - the **free** Merkle hash algebra on a leaf type `α`: digests are the
  binary trees generated by `leaf` and `node`. Both axioms hold by constructor injectivity,
  with no axioms of any kind.
* `Merkle.Free.rootHash_concrete` - a kernel-computed root digest, showing the model really
  evaluates rather than merely existing.

## Why the model does not compress

A real deployment wants digests *shorter* than the data: `β = Fin (2 ^ 256)`, say. But a
collision-free `leaf : α → β` is injective, so it needs `|α| ≤ |β|`; and a collision-free
`node : β → β → β` is injective on `β × β`, so it needs `|β|² ≤ |β|`. For finite `β` with
`|β| ≥ 2` that inequality already fails. So **no finite digest type of bounded size can
satisfy `MerkleHash` for an unbounded leaf type**. Assuming absolute injectivity for a
concrete fixed-width hash would contradict the counting results in `Merkle/Security.lean`.

Real hashes instead use `MerkleSig`, which carries no injectivity assumptions. The constructive
reductions in `Merkle/Security.lean` show that distinct equal-root trees or accepted false leaf
openings yield a collision in that signature. They do not assert that finding collisions is
computationally infeasible, or give probabilistic security bounds.

The free algebra here supplies a concrete model of the ideal injectivity assumptions; its
digests grow with the input tree. The axiom audit of the Merkle theorems permits only
`propext` and `Quot.sound`.
-/

namespace Merkle.Free

/-- The **free** Merkle hash algebra on `α`: the smallest type with an injective `leaf` and an
injective `node`. Digests grow with the tree instead of being compressed, which is exactly
what makes collision-freeness provable rather than assumed. -/
inductive Dig (α : Type) : Type where
  /-- The digest of a leaf carrying `α`. -/
  | leaf : α → Dig α
  /-- The digest of an internal node combining two subtree digests. -/
  | node : Dig α → Dig α → Dig α
  deriving DecidableEq

namespace Dig

/-- `Dig` is a `NodeAlgebra`: pairs of digests are combined by an injective constructor. -/
instance : NodeAlgebra (Dig α) where
  node := Dig.node
  collFree {_a _b _c _d} h := ⟨Dig.node.inj h |>.1, Dig.node.inj h |>.2⟩

/-- `Dig` is a `MerkleHash α`: `leaf` and `node` are both injective, by construction.

This is the model that discharges the assumptions of every theorem in the library. -/
instance : MerkleHash α (Dig α) where
  node := Dig.node
  collFree {_a _b _c _d} h := ⟨Dig.node.inj h |>.1, Dig.node.inj h |>.2⟩
  leaf := Dig.leaf
  leafInjective := fun {_x _y} h => Dig.leaf.inj h

end Dig

/-- Sanity check: the free algebra really is a `MerkleHash`, so `rootHash` is available. -/
example : MerkleHash Nat (Dig Nat) := inferInstance

/-- The root digest of a concrete depth-two tree, computed by the kernel. -/
theorem rootHash_concrete :
    rootHash (α := Nat) (β := Dig Nat) (Tree.node (Tree.node (Tree.leaf 1) (Tree.leaf 2))
      (Tree.node (Tree.leaf 3) (Tree.leaf 4))) =
      Dig.node (Dig.node (Dig.leaf 1) (Dig.leaf 2)) (Dig.node (Dig.leaf 3) (Dig.leaf 4)) := rfl

end Merkle.Free
